  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-122700",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2)^{2}+3(2)=10, \\\\ y & =1+\\sqrt{2(2)}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(10,3).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}}{2t+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}(2t+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=2} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2}(2\\cdot 2+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(10,3)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{1}{14}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{14}(x-10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{14}(0-10) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ y & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(0,\\,\\frac{16}{7})$ is the $y$-intercept.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
